WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:09.760
In 2017, this painting attributed to Leonardo da&nbsp;
Vinci was sold for 450 million dollars at auction.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.800
Because of this, the image traveled around the&nbsp;
world. But what very few people know is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.200
this painting holds a mystery that experts have&nbsp;
been trying to solve for more than 500 years.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.840
If we look at the lower right corner of&nbsp;
this painting, Christ holds in his hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.000
what appears to be a crystal sphere&nbsp;
that allows us to see through it.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.800
Additionally, inside this sphere,&nbsp;
we can see three small dots that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.800
for some reason, Leonardo included for us to see.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.760
What meaning could this mysterious object&nbsp;
that Christ holds in his hands have? And why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.200
after 500 years, have experts&nbsp;
been unable to solve its meaning?

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.440
According to the numerous studies that&nbsp;
have been carried out on this painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.200
it is said that Leonardo da Vinci painted&nbsp;
it around the year 1500. Its original name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.600
is Salvator Mundi, which in English&nbsp;
translates to Savior of the World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.200
and it is probably the most controversial&nbsp;
painting in the entire history of modern art.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.040
In it we see Christ facing forward, dressed&nbsp;
in blue Renaissance robes. His right hand is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:29.280
raised in a sign of blessing, while his left holds&nbsp;
that mysterious transparent crystal sphere which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.680
at first, we might think represents the&nbsp;
celestial sphere of the heavens. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.800
face is framed by auburn curls, and his&nbsp;
expression is serene and impenetrable.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.480
But beyond what we see in the work, its history&nbsp;
is as extraordinary as the painting itself.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.920
For centuries it was lost. It passed through&nbsp;
the hands of King Charles I of England in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.560
seventeenth century, and then disappeared from&nbsp;
the records completely. It resurfaced in 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.960
when it was purchased at a small auction&nbsp;
in the United States for just over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.160
a thousand dollars, cataloged as a&nbsp;
simple copy by a follower of Leonardo.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:12.320
However, after a deep restoration and years&nbsp;
of analysis, in 2011 the National Gallery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:18.560
in London included it in its major Leonardo&nbsp;
exhibition as an authentic work by the master.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:25.182
And finally, in 2017, it was sold at a&nbsp;
Christie's auction in New York for four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.182 --> 00:02:30.960
hundred and fifty million dollars. Thus becoming&nbsp;
the most expensive painting ever sold in history.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.280
But now let us return to the&nbsp;
mystery surrounding this painting.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.840
You may not have noticed it until now, but&nbsp;
there is an error in the sphere Christ is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.560
holding. The first to point out this&nbsp;
problem publicly was Walter Isaacson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.560
the famous biographer of Leonardo, in&nbsp;
2017. According to his observations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.160
Leonardo did not paint the distortion that would&nbsp;
occur when looking through a solid crystal sphere.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.200
Let us keep in mind that if the sphere were&nbsp;
solid crystal, the palm of Christ's hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.480
touching it would look exactly as it is painted.&nbsp;
But floating inside the sphere there should be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.640
reduced and inverted image of his robes and&nbsp;
his arm. Something more or less like this.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.400
So there is something quite puzzling here.&nbsp;
If we look closely, we can see that Leonardo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.080
painted the rest of the sphere with beautiful&nbsp;
scientific precision. At a certain point we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.200
even see three small bright dots inside&nbsp;
this sphere. These dots are inclusions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.440
and they would be the small cavities&nbsp;
that appear in natural rock crystal.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.320
There is even evidence that at that exact&nbsp;
time, Leonardo had examined rock crystals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:44.160
as a favor for Isabella d'Este, one of the&nbsp;
most powerful women in Italy. So Leonardo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:50.880
knew the material perfectly, and painted its&nbsp;
microscopic imperfections with total accuracy.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.880
However, by painting the sphere without&nbsp;
distorting anything, a big question arises.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.720
How is it possible that the greatest scholar of&nbsp;
optics of his time made such a basic mistake?

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.720
The short answer to this question is&nbsp;
that it probably was not a mistake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.160
And the theories that attempt&nbsp;
to explain it are fascinating.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.120
But before telling you about these theories, I&nbsp;
would like to remind you that if you would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.240
to have this painting in your collection,&nbsp;
you can now purchase it from our catalog.

